{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness, specifically whether a lottery is left-skewed by comparing the probability mass on favorable (positive) outcomes versus unfavorable (zero) outcomes. A left-skewed lottery has a greater probability assigned to favorable outcomes (higher payoff) than to unfavorable ones. In this case, both lotteries have a positive outcome (6000 or 3000 Euros) and a zero outcome. Lottery A has a 0.1% chance of 6000 Euros and 99.9% chance of 0. Lottery B has a 0.2% chance of 3000 Euros and 99.8% chance of 0. To assess skewness: a lottery is more left-skewed if more probability is on the positive outcome (i.e., the favorable one). Thus, Lottery A has a 0.1% chance on a large positive outcome (6000 Euros), while B has 0.2% on a smaller positive outcome (3000 Euros). Since the favorable outcome in A is higher in value and occurs with a lower probability, but the probability mass on the positive outcome is still 0.1% (vs. 0.2% in B), we compare the proportion of probability on the favorable outcome. A is less likely to win (0.1% vs. 0.2%), but the payoff is higher. However, for skewness, the key is whether more probability is on the favorable side. Both have a small favorable probability, but A has 0.1% vs. B’s 0.2%, so A has less probability on favorable outcomes. Therefore, B has more probability on favorable outcomes (0.2% > 0.1%), even though the payoff is smaller. Thus, B is more left-skewed. The decision reason would prefer the more left-skewed lottery, which is B. The information is fully derivable from the lotteries' outcomes and probabilities.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report states: 'Bei gewinnbringenden Optionen waren unwahrscheinlich, der potenzielle Gewinn in A jedoch höher.' This translates to: 'Winning options were unlikely, but the potential gain in A was higher.' The individual acknowledges that winning is rare in both options and emphasizes that the potential gain in A (6000 Euros) is higher than in B (3000 Euros). The individual does not discuss probabilities, does not mention skewness, nor does the individual compare the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. The focus is solely on the magnitude of the potential gain, not on the probability distribution or skewness. The individual does not consider the proportion of probability mass on the favorable outcome or the shape of the distribution. Thus, the deliberation process centers on the size of the potential payoff, not on skewness or the distribution of probabilities.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness — specifically, the proportion of probability mass on favorable outcomes — and preferring the more left-skewed lottery. In this case, B has a higher probability (0.2% vs. 0.1%) on a positive outcome, even though the payoff is smaller, making B more left-skewed. However, the verbal report does not mention probability distribution, skewness, or any comparison of the probability mass on favorable outcomes. Instead, it focuses solely on the higher potential gain in A. Therefore, the individual did not consider the skewness of the lotteries or apply the rule of preferring the more left-skewed lottery. The justification in the report is based on payoff magnitude, not distributional properties. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

